# NOBORU DATABASE RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all database design, migrations, repository access, and Supabase configuration within Noboru.

Every table, migration, policy, repository, and query must follow this document and docs/database-schema.md.

If implementation and docs/database-schema.md diverge, the schema document must be updated before the change is considered complete.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* A single authoritative database schema
* Safe, auditable, recoverable educational data
* Row Level Security on all user-owned resources
* Repository-only access from application code
* Predictable migrations and versioning
* Long-term scalability of Noboru data

Educational progress is sacred.

The database is the source of truth.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Canonical Schema

* docs/database-schema.md is authoritative
* No new table, column, index, or relationship without updating the schema doc
* Educational domains own their tables; gamification consumes, never owns, educational progress

Primary Keys

* Use uuid for all primary keys
* Do not introduce serial or integer primary keys for domain entities

Standard Columns

Every table must include:

* id
* created_at
* updated_at

Recommended:

* deleted_at for soft deletes where appropriate

Timestamps

* Store timestamps in UTC
* Never store local user time in core tables

Migrations

* All schema changes live in supabase/migrations/
* Migrations must be idempotent in intent and reviewable
* Never mutate production schema manually outside migration files
* Include RLS policies in the same migration when introducing user-owned tables

Row Level Security

* RLS is required on every user-owned resource
* Policies must enforce ownership and role boundaries
* Application authorization is required in addition to RLS, not instead of it

Repository Access

* All application database access goes through repositories
* Forbidden in components, pages, hooks, and UI logic:
  supabase.from("table") direct queries

Naming

* Tables: snake_case plural where established by schema
* Columns: snake_case
* Foreign keys must be explicit and indexed when queried

Educational Integrity

* Learning history must be auditable and recoverable
* Gamification tables must not become the authority for mastery or progress
* Educational systems must never depend on gamification tables

Supabase

* Use Supabase Auth as the authentication source
* Service role keys are server-only
* Client keys are public-only with RLS protection

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Repositories

* One repository per domain aggregate where possible
* Methods express intent: getDueReviews, upsertProgress, findByUserId
* Return typed records mapped to domain types
* Handle not-found and constraint errors explicitly

Queries

* Select only required columns
* Paginate large result sets
* Index columns used in filters, joins, and ordering
* Avoid N+1 query patterns

Migrations

* Name migrations descriptively
* Include up/down clarity in review even if tool supports one direction
* Document breaking changes in migration comments

Policies

* Default deny for user-owned data
* Separate read and write policies when needed
* Test policies against typical user and admin roles

Types

* Database row types map to feature domain types at repository boundary
* Do not leak raw database shapes into UI

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Database work is successful when:

* docs/database-schema.md matches the implemented schema
* New user-owned tables ship with RLS in the same migration
* All access from app code flows through repositories
* UUID primary keys and standard timestamps are present
* Educational progress remains authoritative and independent from gamification
* Queries are typed, indexed, and scoped to user ownership
* Migrations are reproducible across environments

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Database quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* UI or pages query Supabase directly
* Tables added without schema documentation
* Missing RLS on user-owned data
* Integer primary keys for domain entities
* Gamification tables storing authoritative learning progress
* Business logic inside repositories
* Manual production edits bypassing migrations
* Unindexed filters on high-traffic queries
* Local timestamps stored as canonical values

When failure criteria appear, stop feature work and restore schema discipline first.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct repository method

reviewRepository.listDueItems(userId) applies user scope and returns typed ReviewItem[].

Correct migration

supabase/migrations/20260101000000_add_review_events.sql creates table, indexes, and RLS policies together.

Correct schema alignment

Adding vocabulary_tags updates docs/database-schema.md in the same change set.

Incorrect UI access

useEffect(() => { supabase.from("kanji").select("*") }, []) in a component.

Incorrect authority model

Storing SRS interval authority only in achievements tables.

Incorrect repository

Repository calculates league rank or lesson completion rules.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Direct database access from UI

Database queries inside hooks used by components

Migrations without RLS for user-owned tables

Schema changes without documentation updates

Repositories returning untyped any rows

Using gamification tables as source of truth for mastery

Hard deletes of educational history without audit strategy

Service role key usage in client code

God repositories spanning unrelated domains

Storing secrets or tokens in database tables unnecessarily

